course: dictionary
lesson: 10520
title: What is a typeface?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A typeface is the design of a set of letters, numbers and signs. Times New Roman, Arial and Comic Sans are all typefaces. Each one has the same letters, but each looks different, like a different handwriting style.
explain: Context helps you spot the meaning. «Choose a clear typeface so your poster is easy to read.» Clear and easy to read tell you that a typeface can make text easier or harder to read. A good typeface is one you can read quickly from across the room.
explain: Some words are built from parts you know. «A sans-serif typeface» has no serifs, the little feet on the ends of letters. Sans means without, so sans-serif means without little feet. «A serif typeface» has those little feet.
fact: A typeface is a design for letters, numbers and signs.
fact: Examples of typefaces are Times New Roman, Arial and Comic Sans.
fact: «Serif» means the little feet on the ends of some letters, and «sans-serif» means without them.
fact: «A bold typeface» has thick letters, which can make a heading stand out.
fact: «The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog» is a pangram: it uses every letter of the alphabet, so it shows what a typeface looks like.
exercise: What is a typeface? | A person who types very fast | A computer that prints paper | *A design for a set of letters, numbers and signs | Pencil case for your pens
exercise: «Choose a sans-serif typeface for your poster.» What does sans-serif mean? | In bold capital letters only | With big feet on the letters | Written by hand | *Without little feet on the letters
exercise: Why might you choose a clear typeface for a poster? | So the letters cannot be seen | So the text is hard to read | *So people can read the words easily | So the paper is blank
test: Which of these is a typeface? | Keyboard | Paper | *Arial | Pencil
test: What does «a serif typeface» have? | Only numbers | Only pictures | No lines at all | *Little feet on the ends of the letters
exam: «The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog» is a pangram; why do people use it? | To say hello in a letter | To learn to draw foxes | *To test keyboards and show what a typeface looks like | To count the words in a book
